🚽 Test case: round-23 review finding #1, a third base type -- the
🚽 review specifically warned that "pointer-width accidentally equals
🚽 some scalar widths on some targets and not others" (native x86-64:
🚽 uintptr_t is 8 bytes; wasm32: 4), so a fix verified only against
🚽 `rizz *` (int, 4 bytes on both targets) could still hide a stride bug
🚽 for a base type whose width diverges from uintptr_t differently.
🚽 `chad *` (float, always 4 bytes, same as wasm32's uintptr_t but half
🚽 of native's) exercises a genuinely different scalar width than the
🚽 int-array fixture.
skibidi main {
    chad a = 1.5;
    chad b = 2.5;
    chad *ptrs[2];
    ptrs[0] = &a;
    ptrs[1] = &b;
    yappin("%.1f %.1f\n", *ptrs[0], *ptrs[1]);
    *ptrs[1] = 9.5;
    yappin("%.1f %.1f\n", a, b);
    bussin 0;
}
